// A raw-backed enum's case value folds literals and operators over them, and
// refuses a name — DF-232c's deliberate boundary.
//
// A `static` (or another enum's case) resolves by stamping leaves against a
// table `_collect_const_statics` builds in DECLARATION order. An enum's cases
// are pinned by its own declaration, which is why every enum is read BEFORE any
// static: a flag-enum static like `static RW: UInt8 = Perm.Read | Perm.Write`
// needs the case numbers to already exist. Naming a static from a case value
// would therefore be a forward reference into a table that has not been built,
// so it is refused by name rather than half-supported.
//
// The refusal is also the ONLY error for this case. `raw_value` stays unfolded,
// and registration's "needs an explicit value" check skips a case that wrote an
// expression — saying a value is missing when the source plainly has one would
// contradict the code the reader is looking at.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: raw value for case `A` of enum `Ref` is not a compile-time constant
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: It may not name a `static` or another enum's case

static BASE: Int = 4

enum Ref: UInt8 {
    case A = BASE,
}

func main() {
    print("{}", Ref.A as UInt8)
}
